WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
Today we're covering the basics of reinforcement learning.

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:09.000
Unlike supervised learning, there's no dataset of labeled examples handed to the agent up front.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:14.500
Instead, an agent interacts with an environment, taking actions and observing what happens.

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:19.000
After each action, the environment returns a new state and a scalar reward.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:24.000
The agent's goal is to choose actions that maximize the total reward it collects over time.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:29.500
A policy is the agent's strategy: a mapping from states to actions, or to a distribution over actions.

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:35.000
A value function estimates how much future reward the agent can expect from a given state.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:40.000
The action-value function, often written Q, estimates expected future reward for taking a specific action in a state.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:45.500
One central tension in reinforcement learning is exploration versus exploitation.

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:51.000
Exploitation means choosing the action currently believed to be best, based on what's been learned so far.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:56.500
Exploration means trying an action specifically to learn more about the environment, even if it looks worse right now.

00:00:56.500 --> 00:01:02.000
An epsilon-greedy policy exploits most of the time, but explores a random action with small probability epsilon.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:07.500
Temporal-difference learning updates a value estimate using the difference between consecutive predictions, without waiting for the episode to end.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:13.000
Q-learning is a temporal-difference method that learns the action-value function directly, independent of the policy being followed.

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:18.000
Because it learns off-policy, Q-learning can reuse experience collected under an older or more exploratory policy.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:23.500
A discount factor, usually written gamma and between zero and one, controls how much the agent values future reward compared to immediate reward.

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:28.500
A discount factor close to zero makes the agent short-sighted; close to one makes it weigh distant rewards almost as much as immediate ones.

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:33.000
When the state space is too large to hold a table of values, a function approximator, such as a neural network, estimates the value function instead.

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:38.000
That combination of deep neural networks with reinforcement learning is usually called deep reinforcement learning.
